        "bodyTr": "The funeral procession of Choi Bool-am, the \"Father of the Nation,\" will be held solemnly today. The significance of his passing extends beyond the retirement of a veteran actor—it represents the loss of a familiar face that embodied modern Korean history and the lives of ordinary people. He was a figure who showed us what kind of support and what kind of adult we longed for in this rapidly changing world. As Chairman Kim of Yangjchon Village in \"The Idyllic Farm,\" which aired for 22 years from 1980 to 2002, he was a steadfast father figure who preserved a spiritual hometown for those who had left the countryside due to industrialization and urbanization. As a patriarch, he was stern yet carried warmth in his heart, caring for his family and village neighbors. The authority he displayed as a head of household stemmed from his sense of responsibility toward family and community. His rough-hewn manner of speaking and his hearty, booming laughter carried with them the weight and affection of fathers of that era. He embodied the ideal image of a patriarch that the public yearned for. As Inspector Park in \"Police Subtraction,\" which aired for 18 years from 1971 to 1989, he confronted the times in the heart of the city. Whenever crimes born of economic necessity—reflecting the shadows of compressed growth—appeared, he pursued the perpetrators relentlessly and captured them, yet he did not end his mission there. He hated the sin but not the sinner, and he wrestled with the circumstances behind the crime and human dignity. Choi Bool-am's Inspector Park, who heeded his mother's wishes and never wore a gun or handcuffs even on screen, was another symbol of an ideal adult. Chairman Kim of the countryside and Inspector Park of the city reflected both sides of an era and sustained the people's hearts. Choi Bool-am outside of drama was no different. In the documentary \"The Korean Table,\" which he hosted for 14 years beginning in 2011, he did not merely taste humble food from across the nation but listened carefully to people's lives. When the \"Choi Bool-am Series,\" which mockingly caricatured him, became a trend in the 1990s, he accepted it with his characteristic hearty laughter and broke down the wall between himself and the younger generation. His decades of support for children and youth through the Green Umbrella Foundation also proved that his warmth was not limited to words and acting. He was called the \"Father of the Nation\" not simply because he played father roles well. It was because he was an adult who was stern yet kind, who upheld principles while understanding people, and who stood beside ordinary citizens rather than lecturing from on high, sharing their tears and laughter. What he left behind is ultimately a longing for such an adult.",
